\subsection{Multimodal Embedding Models}

Multimodal embedding models are a type of machine learning model that can learn representations of data from multiple modalities, such as text, images, and audio. These models have been used in a variety of applications, including image and video classification, object detection, and speech recognition. In recent years, there has been a growing interest in developing universal multimodal embedding models that can handle a wide range of tasks. These models are typically trained on large datasets and can be fine-tuned for specific tasks. There are several challenges associated with developing universal multimodal embedding models, including the need for large amounts of training data, the difficulty of training models that can handle multiple modalities, and the need for efficient inference. Despite these challenges, there has been significant progress in this area in recent years, and universal multimodal embedding models are now being used in a variety of applications, including image and video classification, object detection, and speech recognition. <|section_end|>

